[Subclasses weren't what I was arguing for here, (1) was intended only
to be taken together with (2).]

> -(2) Any numbering changes are done in the individual documents.
>
> To be discouraged at best. Numbering is to be handled by the
> document style. The only excuse for changing it is when using a
> generic document style.

Why? You didn't explain the reasons.

> Hard references should be checked in any event because of
> problems with text being edited and numbers changing. See above.

Yes and no. At the point when an electronic document is turned over to
a publisher, the publisher usually prefers to minimize changes, thus
the chance of numbers changing is quite small. (Except for page refs,
but hard-coding of page refs is rarer than hard-coding of item refs,
and more obviously counterproductive.)

